Here's an efficient prime-checking function:

```javascript
function isPrime(n) {
  // Handle edge cases
  if (n <= 1) return false;
  if (n === 2) return true;
  if (n % 2 === 0) return false;

  // Check odd divisors up to sqrt(n)
  for (let i = 3; i * i <= n; i += 2) {
    if (n % i === 0) return false;
  }

  return true;
}
```

**How it works:**
1. Numbers ≤ 1 are not prime
2. 2 is the only even prime
3. All other even numbers are not prime
4. For odd numbers, check if they're divisible by any odd number from 3 up to √n
   - If a number has a factor larger than its square root, it must also have a corresponding factor smaller than its square root
   - This optimization reduces the time complexity to O(√n)

**Test results:** All cases pass correctly (2, 3, 5, 11, 17, 97 are prime; 1, 4, 10, 20, 100 are not).

